% ============================================================================
% angle-pairs — the defining condition of each named PAIR-of-angles relationship
% (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOMETRY).
% ============================================================================
% A geometry facts library: given the NAME of a relationship between TWO angles,
% recall the condition that defines it — "complementary angles sum to 90°,
% supplementary angles sum to 180°." Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? angle_pair(complementary, $Cond)     % sum_to_90
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `angle_pair(relationship, condition)` carrying the table's citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% condition WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% name that is not a pair-relationship in the table.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% DISTINCT FROM angle-types.  A sibling library (geometry/angle-types) classifies
% a SINGLE angle by its own measure — acute (< 90°), right (= 90°), obtuse
% (> 90°).  THIS library is about a RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN TWO angles: whether the
% pair is complementary, supplementary, vertical, or adjacent.  `acute` is a
% single-angle TYPE, not a pair relationship, so it is deliberately ABSENT here —
% asking `? angle_pair(acute, $C)` abstains.  Do not confuse the two tables: one
% answers "what kind of angle is this?", the other "how are these two angles
% related?".
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
%
% Truth table (relationship -> defining condition, snake_case atom):
%
%     relationship    | condition               | in words
%     ----------------+-------------------------+-------------------------------
%     complementary   | sum_to_90               | the two measures sum to 90°
%     supplementary   | sum_to_180              | the two measures sum to 180°
%     vertical        | equal                   | opposite angles at intersecting
%                     |                         |   lines; they are equal
%     adjacent        | share_side_and_vertex   | share a common vertex and one
%                     |                         |   common side
%
% Backward query.  The relation runs in reverse — given a condition, recall which
% relationship it defines:
%
%     ? angle_pair($Rel, sum_to_180)         % supplementary
%
% Abstention property.  The table is CLOSED: only the four grounded pair
% relationships resolve.  A single-angle type (`acute`, `right`, `obtuse`), an
% unrelated word, or a relationship the source does not define (e.g. an
% ungrounded `explementary`) binds nothing and the engine abstains — it never
% fabricates a condition.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance.  Every relationship→condition pair below is copied from ONE primary
% teaching reference — the OpenStax "Contemporary Mathematics" chapter 10.2
% "Angles", hosted on Mathematics LibreTexts — which defines all four pair
% relationships on a single page.  The verbatim defining sentence for each row is
% quoted in its inline `%` span comment; the representative sentence in the
% `source` envelope is the complementary definition, and the `locator` is that
% one page.  `trust consensus` — Mathematics LibreTexts is a widely used,
% peer-reviewed open textbook platform (a consensus reference), not a primary
% research artifact, so it earns the consensus tier rather than authoritative.
% Nothing here is asserted from memory; the atoms encode the source's OWN wording
% ("sum of their measures equals 90°" -> sum_to_90; "one common side ... vertex"
% -> share_side_and_vertex).  (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table angle_pair {
    columns relationship, condition

    % "Two angles are complementary if the sum of their measures equals 90°."
    row (complementary, sum_to_90)
    % "Two angles are supplementary if the sum of their measures equals 180°."
    row (supplementary, sum_to_180)
    % "When two lines intersect, the opposite angles are called vertical angles,
    %  and vertical angles have equal measure."
    row (vertical, equal)
    % "Two angles with the same starting point or vertex and one common side are
    %  called adjacent angles."
    row (adjacent, share_side_and_vertex)

    source "Two angles are complementary if the sum of their measures equals 90°."
    locator "https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Applied_Mathematics/Contemporary_Mathematics_(OpenStax)/10:__Geometry/10.02:_Angles"
    trust consensus
}
